 are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n435,RESIDUALS,\"**Residuals in Regression Analysis**\n\nResiduals are a fundamental concept in regression analysis, defined as the differences between observed values and the values predicted by a regression model. Mathematically, for a given observation \\(i\\), the residual \\(e_i\\) can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\ne_i = y_i - \\hat{y}_i\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_i\\) represents the observed value and \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) denotes the predicted value from the regression model. The residuals serve multiple purposes in econometrics, primarily in assessing the accuracy and fit of the model.\n\nOne of the primary uses of residuals is to evaluate the model's fit. By analyzing the residuals, researchers can determine how well the regression model captures the underlying data patterns. A good model will have residuals that are randomly distributed around zero, indicating that the model's predictions are unbiased. Conversely, systematic patterns in the residuals may suggest that the model is misspecified or that important variables are omitted.\n\nResiduals are also crucial in diagnostic checking, particularly in the context of testing for heteroscedasticity, which refers to the condition where the variance of the residuals is not constant across all levels of the independent variable(s). If residuals exhibit a pattern (e.g., increasing or decreasing spread), it may indicate heteroscedasticity, which can violate the assumptions of ordinary least squares (OLS) regression and lead to inefficient estimates and biased inference.\n\nIn addition to assessing model fit and diagnosing issues like heteroscedasticity, residuals are employed in various statistical tests and graphical analyses. For instance, residual plots, which graph the residuals against predicted values or independent variables, can visually reveal patterns that suggest model inadequacies. Furthermore, statistical tests such as the Breusch-Pagan test or the White test utilize residuals to formally assess the presence of heteroscedasticity.\n\nThe importance of residuals extends beyond mere diagnostics; they are integral to the estimation process itself. In OLS regression, the goal is to minimize the sum of squared residuals, which is expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Minimize } \\sum_{i=1}^{n} e_i^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2\n\\]\n\nThis minimization leads to the best linear unbiased estimates (BLUE) of the regression coefficients under the Gauss-Markov assumptions.\n\nIn summary, residuals are the differences between observed and predicted values in regression analysis, serving as a critical tool for assessing model accuracy, diagnosing potential issues such as heteroscedasticity, and guiding the estimation process. Their analysis is essential for ensuring the reliability and validity of regression models, making them a cornerstone of econometric practice.\",21\r\n79,SPECIFICATION ERRORS,\"**Specification Errors**\n\nSpecification errors refer to the inaccuracies that arise when a statistical model is incorrectly formulated. These errors can significantly impact the reliability of the model's estimates and predictions. Specifically, specification errors occur when the model fails to accurately represent the underlying data-generating process, which can lead to biased or inconsistent estimates of the parameters involved. \n\nThe consequences of specification errors are multifaceted. Firstly, they can result in biased estimates, meaning that the estimated coefficients do not reflect the true relationships between the variables. This bias can stem from various sources, such as omitting relevant variables, including irrelevant ones, or mischaracterizing the functional form of the model. For instance, if a linear model is used to represent a nonlinear relationship, the estimates derived from this model will likely be misleading.\n\nSecondly, specification errors can lead to inconsistent estimates. In econometrics, an estimator is said to be consistent if, as the sample size increases, it converges in probability to the true parameter value. When a model is mis-specified, the estimators may not converge to the true values, even with large samples, thus undermining the reliability of inference drawn from the model.\n\nMoreover, specification errors can adversely affect the model's predictive power. An incorrectly specified model may yield predictions that deviate significantly from actual outcomes, leading to erroneous conclusions and potentially misguided policy recommendations. This is particularly critical in applied econometrics, where accurate predictions are essential for decision-making.\n\nTo mitigate the risk of specification errors, econometricians employ various diagnostic tests and model selection criteria. Techniques such as the Ramsey RESET test can help identify functional form misspecifications, while information criteria like the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) and the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) assist in selecting the most appropriate model among competing specifications. Additionally, conducting robustness checks and sensitivity analyses can provide insights into the stability of the model's estimates under different specifications.\n\nIn summary, specification errors are a crucial concern in econometric modeling, as they can lead to biased, inconsistent estimates and inaccurate predictions. Understanding the nature of these errors, their implications, and the methods available to detect and correct them is essential for ensuring the validity and reliability of econometric analyses.\",5\r\n1534,AR(1) SCHEME,The AR(1) scheme is a statistical model that describes a time series where the current value is based on its immediately preceding value.,1\r\n3521,STUDENTIZED RESIDUAL,\"**Studentized Residual**\n\nThe studentized residual is a crucial concept in regression analysis, particularly in the context of identifying influential data points and outliers. It is defined as the residual divided by an estimate of its standard deviation. Mathematically, the studentized residual \\( r_i^* \\) for the \\( i \\)-th observation can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nr_i^* = \\frac{e_i}{\\hat{\\sigma}_i}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( e_i \\) is the residual for the \\( i \\)-th observation, and \\( \\hat{\\sigma}_i \\) is the estimated standard deviation of the residuals. This transformation allows for a standardized measure of how far an observation deviates from the fitted regression line, taking into account the variability of the residuals.\n\nThe importance of studentized residuals lies in their ability to highlight observations that may disproportionately influence the results of a regression analysis. By standardizing the residuals, researchers can more easily identify outliers—data points that lie significantly outside the expected range of values. Observations with studentized residuals greater than 2 or less than -2 are often considered potential outliers, while those exceeding 3 or -3 are typically flagged as highly influential.\n\nThe calculation of studentized residuals is predicated on certain assumptions inherent in regression analysis. These include the assumptions of linearity, independence, homoscedasticity (constant variance of errors), and normality of the error terms. If these assumptions hold, the studentized residuals will follow a t-distribution with \\( n - k - 1 \\) degrees of freedom, where \\( n \\) is the number of observations and \\( k \\) is the number of predictors in the model. This distributional property is essential for making statistical inferences about the significance of the residuals.\n\nHowever, the violation of these assumptions can lead to misleading interpretations of studentized residuals. For instance, if the assumption of homoscedasticity is violated, the estimated standard deviations used in the calculation of studentized residuals may be biased, leading to incorrect conclusions about the presence of outliers. Similarly, if the errors are not normally distributed, the t-distribution assumption may not hold, further complicating the analysis.\n\nIn practice, studentized residuals are often used in conjunction with other diagnostic tools, such as leverage statistics and Cook's distance, to assess the influence of individual data points on the overall regression model. By examining these metrics collectively, researchers can make more informed decisions about the validity of their regression results and the potential need for data transformation or model adjustment.\n\nIn summary, studentized residuals serve as a vital diagnostic tool in regression analysis, enabling the identification of outliers and influential observations. Their calculation involves dividing the residuals by their estimated standard deviations, and they are interpreted within the context of the underlying assumptions of the regression model. Understanding and correctly applying studentized residuals is essential for ensuring the robustness and reliability of regression analyses.\",1\r\n2570,PACF,\"The Partial Autocorrelation Function (PACF) is a crucial tool in time series analysis, specifically designed to measure the correlation between a time series and its own past values while controlling for the effects of intervening observations. This function is particularly useful in identifying the appropriate order of autoregressive models, such as AR(p) models, by providing insights into the direct relationship between a variable and its lagged values.\n\nMathematically, the PACF at lag \\( k \\), denoted as \\( \\phi_{kk} \\), is defined as the correlation between the time series \\( Y_t \\) and its lagged values \\( Y_{t-k} \\) after removing the influence of all intervening lags \\( Y_{t-1}, Y_{t-2}, \\ldots, Y_{t-(k-1)} \\). The PACF can be computed using the following formula:\n\n\\[\n\\phi_{kk} = \\frac{\\text{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-k} | Y_{t-1}, Y_{t-2}, \\ldots, Y_{t-(k-1)})}{\\sqrt{\\text{Var}(Y_t | Y_{t-1}, Y_{t-2}, \\ldots, Y_{t-(k-1)}) \\cdot \\text{Var}(Y_{t-k} | Y_{t-1}, Y_{t-2}, \\ldots, Y_{t-(k-1)})}}\n\\]\n\nThe PACF is particularly important in the context of model selection for time series forecasting. When constructing an autoregressive model, the PACF helps to determine the maximum number of lags to include in the model. Specifically, a significant PACF at lag \\( k \\) indicates that the \\( k \\)-th lag has a direct effect on the current value of the series, while non-significant PACF values beyond lag \\( p \\) suggest that including additional lags would not improve the model.\n\nThe interpretation of the PACF is straightforward: if the PACF cuts off after a certain lag \\( p \\), it suggests that an AR(p) model is appropriate for the time series. Conversely, if the PACF tails off, it may indicate that a more complex model, such as an ARIMA model, could be necessary.\n\nIn terms of assumptions, the PACF relies on the assumption that the time series is stationary, meaning that its statistical properties do not change over time. Stationarity is crucial because non-stationary data can lead to misleading PACF results, potentially resulting in incorrect model specifications. If the assumption of stationarity is violated, it may be necessary to transform the data through differencing or detrending before applying the PACF.\n\nIn summary, the Partial Autocorrelation Function (PACF) is an essential component of time series analysis, providing a method to assess the direct relationships between a time series and its lagged values while controlling for the influence of shorter lags. Its application in model selection, particularly for autoregressive models, underscores its importance in econometric modeling and forecasting. Understanding the PACF, along with its assumptions and implications, is vital for effective time series analysis and accurate model specification.\",3\r\n1618,CUBIC TOTAL COST FUNCTION,The cubic total cost function is a mathematical representation used in econometrics to model the relationship between total cost and output.,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n2061,RESIDUALS,SPECIFICATION ERRORS,Examination of residuals can help identify specification errors in a statistical model,26\r\n1918,AR(1) SCHEME,RESIDUALS,The AR(1) scheme is used to model the relationship between current and past residuals in time series analysis,22\r\n4482,STUDENTIZED RESIDUAL,RESIDUALS,Studentized residuals are derived from residuals and provide a standardized measure to identify influential observations,22\r\n3298,PACF,CORRELOGRAM,PACF is also represented in a correlogram to visualize partial autocorrelations,9\r\n46,SPECIFICATION ERRORS,OUTLIERS,Outliers can cause specification errors by skewing the results of econometric models,8\r\n2022,SPECIFICATION ERRORS,CUBIC TOTAL COST FUNCTION,The cubic total cost function is an example of a model that can suffer from specification errors if not correctly applied,6\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overal